<p>
            Few home buyers need what the L7 costs. The questions are whether you need its 400 lb rating and
            build, and whether you can get them for less.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Other light-commercial, non-folding machines.</strong> <a href="/woodway-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Woodway</a>, <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor</a> and <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a> sell at similar or higher prices, with different warranty structures.</li>
            <li><strong>Heavy-duty home treadmills.</strong> Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for heavy people</a> guide ranks machines rated to 400 lb at a fraction of the price, mostly with shorter warranties.</li>
            <li><strong>Running treadmills under $2,000.</strong> For a single runner of average build, a well-built folding machine such as the <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole F80</a> covers the same speeds and incline with a 60-inch deck.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Landice’s own folding M2 Genesis ($3,999 in our October check) is the cheaper way into the brand,
            covered in our <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Who Should Buy the Landice L7</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy the L7 if durability is the priority.</strong> A 400 lb rating, an aluminium frame and
            lifetime parts cover for home use are the reasons to pay from $5,429.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the L8 instead</strong> if you are tall, run with a long stride, weigh close to 400 lb or
            share the machine with someone much larger.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the L7 RTM</strong> if you or a patient need full-length handrails, a very slow start or
            a reversing belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you need a folding machine, a 60-inch deck at L7 money, or the
            most features per dollar; and in every case price the exact configuration from Landice and a dealer
            before you decide.
          </p>
